I can see what's happening here, Andre. The sparse-checkout created a nested mess. Let me give you the clean way to do this.
The issue: You likely ran clone commands that created vvd_vintage inside an existing vvd_vintage folder, and the sparse-checkout got tangled up.
Clean fix - start fresh:
# 1. Go to your working directory (one level ABOVE where you want vvd_vintage)
cd \\maple.fg.rbc.com\data\Toronto\wrkgrp\wrkgrp16\Marketing

# 2. Delete the messed up folder
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force vvd_vintage

# 3. Clone with sparse checkout from the start
git clone --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/7AVS/vvd_vintage.git

# 4. Enter the repo
cd vvd_vintage

# 5. Tell git you only want the reports folder
git sparse-checkout set reports

# Done. Now you only have /reports
To update later (pull new changes):
cd \\maple.fg.rbc.com\data\Toronto\wrkgrp\wrkgrp16\Marketing\vvd_vintage
git pull
That's it. The key mistake was probably running git clone while already inside a vvd_vintage folder, which created the nesting. The --filter=blob:none --sparse flags on clone set everything up correctly from the start so you don't need the separate init --cone step.
